<div class="src">10-K store tables<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-4">4</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-6">6</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-8">8</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-10">10</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-13">13</a></sup>, Q3 2014 10-Q<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-14">14</a></sup>, April 2015 8-K<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-16">16</a></sup>. The 1996 figure includes 53 McDuff stores.</div>
</div>
<p class="note rv">After April 2015 we have no reliable count of RadioShack-branded stores. General Wireless’s 2017 filing planned to close or hand over 552 stores in its first month, and later RadioShack locations in the U.S. are dealer or franchise stores.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-26">26</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-27">27</a></sup></p>
</div>
</section>

<section id="rivals" class="alt">
<div class="wrap">
<div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-k"><span class="sn">08</span>The rivals</div><h2 class="rv">Squeezed from both sides</h2><p class="lede rv">Big-box stores beat RadioShack on selection and price, the carriers opened their own stores, and the internet took the parts business. Its own partners were also its competitors.</p></div>
<div class="tbl-scroll rv"><table class="cmp">
<tr><th>Company</th><th>Model</th><th>Edge over RadioShack</th><th>Fate</th></tr>
<tr class="me"><td>RadioShack</td><td>About 4,300–5,300 small company stores plus dealers<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-6">6</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-13">13</a></sup></td><td>Proximity, parts, batteries, service</td><td>Chapter 11 in 2015 and a second, as General Wireless, in 2017<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-15">15</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-26">26</a></sup></td></tr>
<tr><td>Best Buy</td><td>Big-box consumer electronics</td><td>Selection and price on TVs, computers and appliances</td><td>Still operating (general knowledge, not from our sources)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Circuit City</td><td>Big-box consumer electronics</td><td>Same as Best Buy</td><td>Liquidated in 2009 (general knowledge, not from our sources)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wireless carriers’ own stores</td><td>Branded stores selling phones and plans</td><td>Control of plans, subsidies and commissions</td><td>Sprint ended up inside RadioShack’s own surviving stores in 2015<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-20">20</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-21">21</a></sup></td></tr>
<tr><td>Amazon and online parts sellers</td><td>Online catalog, home delivery</td><td>Range, price and no trip to the mall</td><td>The online shift is general context; our sources don’t quantify it</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p class="note rv">RadioShack’s 10-Ks name competitors in general terms. The fates of Best Buy and Circuit City are well known but aren’t covered by the sources on this page, so we flag them.</p>
</div>
</section>

<section id="timeline">
<div class="wrap">
<div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-k"><span class="sn">09</span>Timeline</div><h2 class="rv">From Boston radio parts to a brand without stores</h2><p class="lede rv">Filter by thread.</p></div>
<div class="tl-filters" id="tlf"><button class="chip on" data-f="all">All</button><button class="chip" data-f="money">Money</button><button class="chip" data-f="corporate">Corporate</button><button class="chip" data-f="ops">Stores</button><button class="chip" data-f="death">Death &amp; estate</button></div>
<div class="tlv" id="tlv"></div>
</div>
</section>

<section id="people" class="alt">
<div class="wrap">
<div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-k"><span class="sn">10</span>The people</div><h2 class="rv">Founders, owners and five chief executives</h2><p class="lede rv">Titles and dates are from the 10-Ks and 8-Ks unless noted. We include only what a source supports.</p></div>
<div class="people">
<div class="person rv"><h3>Theodore and Milton Deutschmann</h3><div class="role">Founders, 1921</div><p>Opened the first store in Boston, according to the 2020 release from the brand’s owner.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-28">28</a></sup></p></div>
<div class="person rv"><h3>Charles Tandy</h3><div class="role">Bought the chain, 1963</div><p>Head of Tandy Corporation of Fort Worth, which turned the struggling Boston chain profitable within two years.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-18">18</a></sup></p></div>
<div class="person rv"><h3>David Edmondson</h3><div class="role">CEO until Feb 2006</div><p>Resigned in February 2006 after it emerged that he had claimed degrees he didn’t hold.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-17">17</a></sup></p></div>
<div class="person rv"><h3>Julian Day</h3><div class="role">Chairman and CEO from Jul 2006</div><p>Took over after Edmondson. Company stores fell by 505 in 2006, and about 400 headquarters staff were laid off by email that August.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-10">10</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-22">22</a></sup></p></div>
<div class="person rv"><h3>James Gooch</h3><div class="role">CEO, May 2011 – 2012</div><p>Joined as an executive in 2006, served as CFO and became president in January 2011 and CEO in May 2011.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-19">19</a></sup></p></div>
<div class="person rv"><h3>Joseph Magnacca</h3><div class="role">CEO, Feb 2013 – 2015</div><p>Came from Walgreens. Led the final turnaround attempt, including the 2014 Super Bowl ad, and the company into Chapter 11.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-13">13</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-24">24</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-15">15</a></sup></p></div>
<div class="person rv"><h3>Standard General</h3><div class="role">Buyer of 1,743 stores, 2015</div><p>Its affiliate General Wireless bought the stores and co-branded many with Sprint, then filed its own Chapter 11 in March 2017.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-21">21</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-26">26</a></sup></p></div>
<div class="person rv"><h3>Retail Ecommerce Ventures</h3><div class="role">Owner of the brand, 2020–2023</div><p>Founded by Alex Mehr and Tai Lopez, REV bought the RadioShack brands in November 2020 to run them online-first.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-28">28</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-29">29</a></sup></p><div class="then">Then: sold the IP in about 70 countries to Unicomer in 2023.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-30">30</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-31">31</a></sup></div></div>
</div>
</div>
</section>

<section id="press">
<div class="wrap">
<div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-k"><span class="sn">11</span>Press</div><h2 class="rv">Headlines along the way</h2><p class="lede rv">These are headlines as published, not quotations from people.</p></div>
<div class="press">
<div class="clip rv big"><q>RadioShack Layoff Notices Are Sent by E-Mail</q><div class="who"><b>The New York Times</b>, Aug 31, 2006<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-22">22</a></sup></div></div>
<div class="clip rv"><q>RadioShack would like you to call it ‘The Shack’</q><div class="who"><b>USA Today</b>, Aug 5, 2009<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-23">23</a></sup></div></div>
<div class="clip rv"><q>The ’80s called. They want their store back.</q><div class="who"><b>RadioShack Super Bowl ad</b>, Feb 2, 2014<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-24">24</a></sup></div></div>
<div class="clip rv"><q>RadioShack files for bankruptcy; Sprint to take over some stores</q><div class="who"><b>Reuters</b>, Feb 2015<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-25">25</a></sup></div></div>
<div class="clip rv"><q>RadioShack files for second bankruptcy in 2 years</q><div class="who"><b>AP</b>, Mar 2017<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-26">26</a></sup></div></div>
<div class="clip rv"><q>Left for dead, twice, RadioShack gets another shot online</q><div class="who"><b>AP</b>, Nov 2020<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-29">29</a></sup></div></div>
<div class="clip rv big"><q>RadioShack under new ownership again</q><div class="who"><b>Retail Dive</b>, Aug 2023<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-30">30</a></sup></div></div>
</div>
</div>
</section>

<section id="cause" class="alt">
<div class="wrap">
<div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-k"><span class="sn">12</span>Cause of death</div><h2 class="rv">Contributing factors, weighted</h2><p class="lede rv">The weights are our editorial judgment, not a calculation. Click a factor for the evidence.</p></div>
<div class="cod" id="cod"></div>
<div class="disclaimer">How we weight: each factor is scored by how directly the filings tie it to falling gross profit and to the cash drain of 2012–2014. Reasonable analysts could weight these differently.</div>
</div>
</section>

<section id="fork">
<div class="wrap">
<div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-k"><span class="sn">13</span>What if</div><h2 class="rv">Three forks in the road</h2><p class="lede rv">Counterfactuals are speculation by definition. Each one starts from a documented decision.</p></div>
<div class="fork-tabs" id="forkTabs"><button class="on" data-i="0">Shrink in 2006</button><button data-i="1">Stay the parts store</button><button data-i="2">Sell while it was worth something</button></div>
<div class="fork-panel on"><div class="actual"><div class="lbl">What happened</div><p>After cutting 505 company stores in 2006, RadioShack held its footprint near 4,450 for six years. It still had 4,297 at the end of 2013, while sales per store kept falling.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-10">10</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-13">13</a></sup></p></div><div class="whatif"><div class="lbl">What if (speculative)</div><p>Closing another thousand of the weakest stores in 2007–2009, when the company earned about $200M a year and held $500M–$900M in cash, would have cut rent and payroll while there was money to pay lease exit costs. A smaller chain might have reached 2014 without needing a lender’s rescue.</p></div></div>
<div class="fork-panel"><div class="actual"><div class="lbl">What happened</div><p>Wireless rose from 28.7% of sales in 2003 to a 52.4% “mobility” share in 2013, and the 2013 10-K blamed postpaid phones for the drop in gross profit.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-9">9</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-13">13</a></sup></p></div><div class="whatif"><div class="lbl">What if (speculative)</div><p>Leaning into parts, repair and the hobbyist market it was founded on would have meant a much smaller company. But it would have sold goods that carriers and big-box stores didn’t, at margins near the 50% it earned in 2004.</p></div></div>
<div class="fork-panel"><div class="actual"><div class="lbl">What happened</div><p>The share price fell below $3 in 2012 and stayed there. By the end of 2013 equity was $206.4M, down from $1,048.3M in 2009.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-13">13</a></sup></p></div><div class="whatif"><div class="lbl">What if (speculative)</div><p>A sale or merger in 2009–2010, while the shares traded near $20 and the balance sheet was solid, could have returned far more to shareholders than the bankruptcy did. Whether any buyer wanted 4,500 small leases is another question.</p></div></div>
</div>
</section>

<section id="homage" class="alt">
<div class="wrap">
<div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-k"><span class="sn">14</span>Homage</div><h2 class="rv">The look, recreated</h2><p class="lede rv">An original recreation of the feel of a 1990s parts counter: a red header bar, a dark category strip and the wall of labeled drawers. No logo or trademark artwork is reproduced.</p></div>
<div class="homage rs rv">
